given-names></name><name><surname>Cooper</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><name><surname>Todd</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Clayton</surname><given-names>DG</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Detecting disease associations due to linkage disequilibrium using haplotype tags: a class of tests and the determinants of statistical power</article-title><source>Hum. Hered.</source><year>2003</year><volume>56</volume><fpage>18</fpage><lpage>31</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14614235</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B38"><label>38</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>WY</given-names></name><name><surname>Todd</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The usefulness of different density SNP maps for disease association studies of common variants</article-title><source>Hum. Mol. Genet.</source><year>2003</year><volume>12</volume><fpage>3145</fpage><lpage>3149</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14532327</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B39"><label>39</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Klein</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Zeiss</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Chew</surname><given-names>EY</given-names></name><name><surname>Tsai</surname><given-names>JY</given-names></name><name><surname>Sackler</surname><given-names>RS</given-names></name><name><surname>Haynes</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Henning</surname><given-names>AK</given-names></name><name><surname>Sangiovanni</surname><given-names>JP</given-names></name><name><surname>Mane</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Complement factor H polymorphism in age-related macular degeneration</article-title><source>Science</source><year>2005</year><volume>308</volume><fpage>385</fpage><lpage>389</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15761122</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B40"><label>40</label><element-citation publication-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fleiss</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name></person-group><source>Statistical Methods for Rates and Proportions</source><year>2003</year><edition>3rd</edition><publisher-loc>New York, USA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>John Wiley &#x00026; Sons</publisher-name></element-citation></ref></ref-list><app-group><app><title>APPENDIX</title><p>In general, the sample size <italic>n</italic> required to detect the difference between proportions <italic>&#x003c0;</italic><sub>1</sub> and <italic>&#x003c0;</italic><sub>2</sub> by means of a &#x003c7;<sup>2</sup> test can be approximated by<disp-formula id="M12"><label>(A.1)</label><graphic xlink:href="gkm621m12"/></disp-formula>where &#x003c0; = (&#x003c0;<sub>1</sub> + &#x003c0;<sub>2</sub>)/2, &#x003b1; and 1 &#x02212; &#x003b2; are the significance level and power of the applied test, respectively, and <italic>z</italic><sub>1&#x02212;&#x003b1;/2</sub> and <italic>z</italic><sub>1&#x02212;&#x003b2;</sub> denote the corresponding quantiles of the Gaussian distribution (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B40">40</xref>). If <italic>q</italic> is the minor allele frequency of marker <italic>X</italic>, and if the two alleles of marker <italic>Z</italic> are equally frequent, then the corresponding haplotype frequency matrix equals<disp-formula id="M13"><label>(A.2)</label><graphic xlink:href="gkm621m13"/></disp-formula>with<disp-formula id="M14"><label>(A.3)</label><graphic xlink:href="gkm621m14"/></disp-formula>Furthermore, since <italic>Q</italic> = <italic>p</italic><sub>11</sub> + <italic>p</italic><sub>12</sub> = 0.5<italic>&#x003c0;</italic><sub>1</sub> + 0.5(1 &#x02212; <italic>&#x003c0;</italic><sub>1</sub>) = 0.5 and <italic>R</italic> = <italic>p</italic><sub>11</sub> + <italic>p</italic><sub>12</sub> = 0.5&#x003c0;<sub>1</sub> + 0.5&#x003c0;<sub>2</sub>, it follows that<disp-formula id="M15"><label>(A.4)</label><graphic xlink:href="gkm621m15"/></disp-formula>Solving Equations (A.3) and (A.4) for &#x003c0;<sub>1</sub> and &#x003c0;<sub>2</sub> yields &#x003c0;<sub>1</sub> = 1 &#x02212; <italic>q</italic>(1 &#x02013; &#x003c1;) and &#x003c0;<sub>2</sub> = 1 &#x02212; <italic>q</italic>(1 + &#x003c1;), so that &#x003c0; = 1 &#x02212; <italic>q</italic> and &#x003c0;<sub>1</sub> &#x02212; &#x003c0;<sub>2</sub> = 2<italic>q</italic>&#x003c1;. Replacing &#x003c0;<sub>1</sub>, &#x003c0;<sub>2</sub> and <italic>&#x003c0;</italic> by these expressions in formula (A.1) yields<disp-formula id="M16"><label>(A.5)</label><graphic xlink:href="gkm621m16"/></disp-formula>for sufficiently small &#x003c1;. This proves formula (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B10">10</xref>) of the main text.</p></app></app-group></back></article>